# The box proxy's global configuration — the part no tenant owns. Site stanzas
# live one file per project, projects/<name>/sites.caddy, and are pulled
# in by the import at the bottom; this file changes only when a rule that
# applies to every site changes.
#
# No bare-IP stanza anywhere: the origin answers only Cloudflare (the
# DOCKER-USER firewall, firewall.sh in this directory), and Cloudflare dials
# the origin exclusively over 443 (SSL Full (strict)) — visitor HTTP is
# redirected at the edge and never reaches the box.
#
# The domain rides Cloudflare's proxy (orange-cloud), so the TCP peer Caddy
# sees on 443 is a Cloudflare edge node, never the visitor. trusted_proxies
# lets Caddy recover the visitor's IP from the edge's CF-Connecting-IP
# header — and only when the peer really is in Cloudflare's published
# ranges, so a direct-to-IP caller cannot forge an identity by sending the
# header himself. The list (cloudflare.com/ips-v4, fetched 2026-08-10) is one
# of three pinned copies with firewall.sh and the AWS stack's variables.tf;
# scripts/check-cf-ranges.sh keeps them identical in ci and compares them
# with the published list weekly. IPv4 only: every origin record is an A
# record, so Cloudflare dials the box over v4 and ip6tables drops all
# forwarded v6 — the v6 entries carried until 2026-08-30 could never match a
# peer. One line on purpose: the block form (`static { ranges ... }`) adapts
# without error and WITHOUT the ranges (verified 2026-08-30, caddy 2.11.4).
{
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static 173.245.48.0/20 103.21.244.0/22 103.22.200.0/22 103.31.4.0/22 141.101.64.0/18 108.162.192.0/18 190.93.240.0/20 188.114.96.0/20 197.234.240.0/22 198.41.128.0/17 162.158.0.0/15 104.16.0.0/13 104.24.0.0/14 172.64.0.0/13 131.0.72.0/22
		client_ip_headers CF-Connecting-IP
	}
}

# Cloudflare Origin CA certificate (SSL mode: Full (strict)) — a static pair
# issued from the dashboard, trusted only by Cloudflare's edge, which is
# exactly its one job. No ACME: behind the proxy, HTTP-01 can't reach the box,
# and DNS-01 would demand a custom Caddy build. The pair covers
# *.ardabasarici.dev, so every site imports this snippet instead of naming the
# files — where the certificate lives is a box decision, not a tenant's.
(origin_tls) {
	tls /etc/caddy/certs/ardabasarici.dev.pem /etc/caddy/certs/ardabasarici.dev.key
}

# Every tenant's stanzas. compose.yaml mounts the repository's projects/
# directory at /etc/caddy/projects (read-only). One file per tenant, holding
# all of that tenant's sites: Caddy's import allows a single wildcard in the
# pattern (observed on 2.11: `*/*.caddy` is rejected at adapt time), so the
# file name is fixed and the directory is the wildcard. A new tenant is a new
# directory plus a `caddy reload` of this stack — nothing in this file
# changes (runbooks/add-a-tenant.md).
import /etc/caddy/projects/*/sites.caddy
